\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\title{Prerequisites and conventions}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The notes address two readers. One is a mathematician who wants the
neuron models as worked examples of the statements. The other is a
neuroscientist who wants the statements behind phase-plane methods.}

\p{The notes assume calculus of several variables, linear algebra through
eigenvalues and eigenvectors, and the solutions of first-order ordinary
differential equations. The development of those subjects stays in the
books cited here. A derivative of a coordinate along a solution, and
the chain rule that compares two such coordinates, are the calculus
the notes use. An eigenvalue, an eigenvector, and the solutions of
#{\dot x=Ax} for a #{2\times 2} matrix #{A} are the linear algebra.
The time set of an ordinary differential equation is an interval, and
a solution is a differentiable map on that interval. The notes also
assume the words membrane potential and ionic current.
The pair in \ref{fnip-0005} is the first use of
those words for a concrete model: #{V} is the membrane potential, and
the ionic currents are the conductance terms in the voltage equation.
\citet{Chapter 2, p. 25}{izhikevich2007dynamical}
is an account of the ions and the membrane currents, beginning with
sodium, potassium, calcium, and chloride.
The derivative in a [trajectory](fnip-0009) is the first use of the
calculus of several variables.
\citet{§1.0, p. 1}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
is the existence and uniqueness theorem the notes rely on for that
trajectory, and
\citet{§1.1, p. 8}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
is the linear system #{ \dot x = Ax }.
The [dynamical system](fnip-0004) is the first use of a state space.
\citet{Chapter 1, p. 1}{kuznetsov2004elements}
introduces that language, including orbits and phase portraits.
The drawing in \ref{fnip-0011} is the first
use of the phase plane.
\citet{§3.3, p. 51}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
opens that phase plane for the Morris–Lecar pair. A reader who already
knows the Hodgkin–Huxley formalism can pass over the electrophysiology
chapter in that book: the chapter says so at its opening, and it points
to Dayan and Abbott for a fuller account.}

\p{The standing conventions are these. A state is a point of
#{ \mathbb{R}^{n} }. Time is continuous. A vector field is smooth on
the domain named in its statement. A parameter value, and the unit in
which it is printed, are the value and the unit in the book that the
example cites.}

\p{A reader should be able to compute the eigenvalues of a #{2\times 2}
matrix and to sketch the solutions of a linear system in the plane.}
